I'm joined today by Suzanne Kelly she is  author of greening death reclaiming  burial practices and restoring our tie  to the earth she's an independent  scholar  also a farmer in New York's Hudson  Valley beautiful area I've been there  actually and it's a very very very  hipster in some ways sort of like my my  roots so Suzanne let's talk about this  is a really interesting topic burial  practices for humans they sort of  changed over time so paint like the  broad-strokes picture of what the  evolution has been of burial practices  sure well over 150 years ago in the u.s.  we buried our dead in earth friendly  ways you know a simple hole the body was  put in and some kind of a what we would  call today a biodegradable container and  and that was it  people who were buried on the back 40 on  the family farm or in church graveyards  but that all started to change in and  around the 1830s and then what was that  change  well the biggest change was that there  was a rise in in urban populations  collided with new fears around  sanitation this was a time when there  were sanitation reforms happening in  Europe and suddenly in the u.s. the same  thing started to happen there were  diseases that were sweeping through  cities like cholera and yellow fever and  reformers were trying to figure out how  to you know stop people from dying and  getting sick and so they started to  place their attention on really stinky  places in the culture like places of  human waste and of garbage because there  was this belief at that time if  something smelled bad it could actually  cause you like terrible harm it could  actually kill you it was called the  miasma theory and believe it or not some  of those smelly places happened to be  graveyards people like I said were  buried in rural areas out in the back  forty but if not there were in villages  and towns they were they were buried in  church graveyards these were not very  well-kept places they were bodies were  often dug up to make room for other  bodies and so villages and towns and  cities started to place bands on burials  within these locations and they they  began to force the dead outside of to  outside of the village in the town and  the city perimeters this gave rise to  something called the Rural Cemetery  movement which is really nice because  these are beautiful these are beautiful  sent  Terri's almost park-like they grew up in  cities across the budding us we can see  great examples in Boston and Brooklyn  still today but we sometimes forget that  the reason why they actually started at  all was because the dead were sort of  forced there for fear that they could  contaminate us in some way and while  there was some foundation to this  because we didn't really understand how  you know germs affect us or infectious  diseases could really harm us it kind of  snowballed into this idea of the dead  body is something that we have to guard  ourselves against and it sort of made  its way into our burial practices that  was the first thing the second thing was  that the civil war happened and when the  Civil War happens northern soldiers were  dying in the south and great numbers and  suddenly you know people families wanted  burials for for these soldiers for their  sons how to get these bodies back before  decomposition set in prior to this time  embalming even though it had existed for  thousands of years in other parts of the  world was really seen as kind of a fate  worse than death in budding American  culture in European culture it wasn't  something that was done but suddenly  there were new anatomist on the scene  anatomist who were wanting to preserve  cadavers for study and the Union Army  called upon these anatomist for families  who could afford it to have their dead  and bombs to be sent back home for  burial and so you would think that  embalming would have kind of disappeared  when the war ended but it didn't it's  really for two reasons the first is that  Abraham Lincoln when he died his body  was embalmed and it was in bombed and  Riaan bombed that embalm D'Andrea bombed  and traveled across the country in a  railcar so that Americans could actually  gaze upon his body and ultimately gaze  upon this new practice of preservation  some historians have said that it kind  of was symbolic of an overcoming of a  war that had karna donation but as I've  also suggested in my book it was also  representative or symbolic of another  overcoming this preservation you know  this overcoming of nature the other  theater piece of this with embalming was  that the chemical companies and at that  time embalming was mostly made up of  arsenic and other kinds of chemicals  today it's mostly made up of  formaldehyde but the chemical company  has started to set up workshops and  these workshops to be trained in this  new practice of embalming  became our first mortuary schools so the  foundation of our funeral practices in  terms of the funeral industry as we know  it today is foundationally tied to the  development of arterial embalming this  chemical and priming process that is not  required by any state or federal law but  has become common practice so you didn't  really talk much about the influence of  religion in all of this I mean certainly  in many cultures that does play a role  in a lot of these decisions doesn't it  yeah it does and in fact you know if you  look at Jewish burial customs and  Islamic burial customs what we're  calling green burial practices today a  return to the so-called dust to dust  practices the simple burial body of a  body buried inside of some kind of  biodegradable container in kind of a  swift way it's still very much true in  some religious traditions like in  Judaism and in Islam but the burial  practices that came to take shape in the  u.s. were the ones that I just talked  about now embalming and also ultimately  the development of the modern casket and  the burial vault which I can say more  about that trio of practices came to  grow up in and through an industry right  in and through a funeral industry that  was really set apart from from from  religion so the I mean if you think  about the embalming and the chemicals  that are used you think about the wood  or cement or metal or whatever might  also be used to create the burial casket  or a container I don't know of what the  sort of more general umbrella term would  be when you think about all of those  things is cremation really the  eco-friendly thing well that's a great  question I mean when we look at the the  when green barrel advocates look at the  the toxic qualities as you talked about  of the standard American funeral which  includes that trio of practices  embalming the modern casket and the  burial vault the burial vaults as as we  know or maybe we don't know is made is  usually made up of reinforced concrete  sometimes it's made up of plastic  sometimes it's made up of asphalt which  we know is a semi solid form of  petroleum all of which is put in the  ground embalming fluid is a well known  carcinogen that actually is  biodegradable but does pose a threat to  worker health and then of course there's  the modern casket metals  treated hardwoods in some studies of the  soil in silt loam in the in cemeteries  there's been shown to be elevated levels  of things like zinc and cobalt and lead  so all of that is here so yeah you would  think that cremation is is is much  better and I would think that if you I  think that if you talk to most green  burial advocates free cremation is  considered to be the second most  environmentally sound way of disposing  of the dead if you look at the green  burial grounds that have emerged over  the last 20 years in the US and there's  roughly about 150 all of them pretty  much except internment of cremated  remains  if cremation is less than ideal it's  only because anytime you burn something  you pose a threat to soil water and air  and in the u.s. we haven't done such a  great job like they have been doing in  parts of Europe of placing filters on  Crematory is to filter out things like  dioxins and iran's and believe it or not  mercury from dental fillings so you know  we have a little bit of a ways to go  with regard to to those tibet so what  would be sort of at the top of the list  the green dream I guess for burial well  if you're talking simply in terms of  sustainability and lack of pollution  you know simple simple earth burial is  the the least toxic toxic and least  polluting and most sustainable way of  caring for the dead right now that we  have and you know green burial as it  exists in the u.s. right now is whole  body earth burial as I said the use of a  biodegradable container a body is buried  about three and a half four feet deep  all the soil that's taken out of the  ground is put back in the the a burial  mound is typically made and then that  man eventually settles over time and at  its most basic that's what we're seeing  in green burial grounds across the US  but some marine burial grounds actually  state take it a step further and are  focused on the conservation preservation  and restoration of land so if we're  interested if people are interested  really in sustainability and an  environmentalism at that level there is  an effort to really partner with the  land to care for the land and how does  that work well I mean I I know that I've  seen one yeah idea of you can be turned  into a tree but really what it looked  like was that a tree is planted and the  sort of root system will develop around  the body you're not really being turned  into a tree I mean it's sort of a  romanticized way of saying it I think  right yeah that is a product that's on  the market right now but but what I'm  talking about is actually um it's  actually tying the fact of human death  to actually create conservation areas  preservation areas that are used both as  cemeteries but also land that it's cared  for in perpetuity so what we see the  first Green burial ground that opened in  the u.s. called Ramsey Creek in  Westminster South Carolina you know was  open with with that mission with that  purpose of protecting not just the lands  where the burials are happening say that  20 some-odd acres were there burying  bodies but the hundreds of acres that  are surrounding that area some green  burial grounds were that big that can be  used as open space as park space as wild  space for people to enjoy again in  perpetuity we're in all of this in terms  of sustainability does cryopreservation  fit I'm assuming it's not particularly  environmentally friendly particularly  given all of the energy resources that  are required to run those facilities  exactly there is some skepticism by some  green burial advocates that that we have  to look at those at the energy that's  that's put in to some of these so-called  new technologies that are that are that  are on the market or wanting to be on  the market and that certainly would  would fit into one of them I would say  that you know at the purest level green  advocate green burial advocates are  really interested in terms of my own  research and my own on-the-ground work  of developing a green burial ground in  my own community you know really see  this sort of return to the past with a  new environmental consciousness is the  way to go as opposed to these new  technologies that are developing so  everything we've been talking about I  mean this gets back to if this is  interesting to anybody who's listening  or to are watching the interview this  relates to basically estate planning and  you're one one's will right I mean this  is how somebody would have to put into  place a plan if this was appealing to  them yeah you know that's it that's a  great point  and I don't know that one would actually  have to put it into their will they  would have to talk to their you know  their elder law attorney or their estate  planner with respect to that but I think  that what we're seeing with green burial  especially  there are not so many green burial  grounds that exist in the US right now  is that pre-planning is really important  we know that when someone dies there's  sort of a rush to figure out what's  going to happen and you know very often  the funeral machine kind of takes over  and so if people really want to have an  earth burial if they're really  interested in returning to a green  burial ground you know planning ahead  telling your family having the  conversation with your family or with  your attorney is really really important  part of this even with your funeral  director if you're in a state where a  funeral director is required we've been  speaking about the book  greening death reclaiming burial  practices and restoring our tie to the  earth with the book's author Suzanne  Kelly very very interesting work thanks  for sharing it with us thank you

